  "summary": "A traditional Māori meeting house that serves as a center for community gatherings and cultural ceremonies.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate carvings (whakairo) depicting ancestral figures and stories, and a distinctive thatched roof. These elements reflect the Māori worldview connecting people, ancestors, and the natural world.",
  "culturalContext": "The wharenui — Māori meeting house — crystallized in the 1840s-70s as Ngāpuhi and other iwi responded to land loss and mission schooling with monumental carved houses serving as tribal parliament, chapel and genealogy. Its ridgepole is an ancestor's spine, the porch face an ancestor's body; inside, poupou carvings name descent lines. Princess Te Puea Herangi's Tūrangawaewae revival and marae building nationwide keep the form central to Māori life.",
